From e34638151ba7cd35299544902bb2f09f37b31e36 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 19:03:28 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 088/105] zink: let a generated TCS stop naming the nir its
 program freed

zink_shader_tcs_init() builds the generated tessellation control shader out of
the nir the zink_shader already owns, returns that same object to its caller,
and leaves zs->nir pointing at it. gfx_program_init() then serializes it into
the program's blob and ralloc_free()s it with the rest of the stage array, so
the generated TCS keeps naming freed memory for the rest of its life. The
precompile path in zink_compiler.c already clears the pointer after the same
call, and every other place in zink that frees a shader's nir clears it in the
same breath; only this one does not.

The next graphics program built from the same generated TCS passes the
`prog->shaders[MESA_SHADER_TESS_CTRL]->nir` guard on that dangling pointer and
calls zink_shader_tcs_init() again, which walks nir->functions to find the
entrypoint. On this host that read comes back as a null pointer often enough to
crash a run: KHR-GL46.tessellation_shader.single.max_patch_vertices ended two
CTS shards with

  Darwin host fault: signal=11 esr=0x92000006 far=0x0 pc=... lr=...

where esr says a level-2 translation fault on a READ of address 0, on a host
thread that owns no virtual CPU, and the faulting instruction is
zink_shader_tcs_init+0x48 - the `ldr` of the first function node - reached from
gfx_program_init's call to it. (pc and lr keep the same page offsets across
runs, which is what identifies the site through ASLR.)

Clear the pointer where the ownership actually moves. Every later program then
takes the branch it already takes after a precompile and deserializes the blob
zink_shader_tcs_init() wrote before returning, which is the same shader.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_program.c | 12 ++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_program.c b/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_program.c
index 1038af83..ecfab4f8 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_program.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_program.c
@@ -1361,10 +1361,18 @@ gfx_program_init(struct zink_context *ctx, struct zink_gfx_program *prog)
       if (prog->shaders[i]) {
          util_queue_fence_wait(&prog->shaders[i]->precompile.fence);
          /* this may have already been precompiled for separate shader */
-         if (prog->shaders[i]->info.stage == MESA_SHADER_TESS_CTRL && prog->shaders[i]->non_fs.is_generated && prog->shaders[MESA_SHADER_TESS_CTRL]->nir)
+         if (prog->shaders[i]->info.stage == MESA_SHADER_TESS_CTRL && prog->shaders[i]->non_fs.is_generated && prog->shaders[MESA_SHADER_TESS_CTRL]->nir) {
             zink_shader_tcs_init(screen, prog->shaders[MESA_SHADER_TESS_CTRL], nir[MESA_SHADER_TESS_EVAL], &nir[i]);
-         else
+            /* the nir it returns IS the shader's own, and this function frees
+             * it below once it is in the program's blob, so the shader has to
+             * stop naming it - as the precompile path already does. The
+             * serialized blob zink_shader_tcs_init() leaves behind is what
+             * every later program deserializes instead.
+             */
+            prog->shaders[MESA_SHADER_TESS_CTRL]->nir = NULL;
+         } else {
             nir[i] = zink_shader_deserialize(screen, prog->shaders[i]);
+         }
       } else {
          nir[i] = NULL;
       }
